{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfy7NiQ3x2c2uTiydRMk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Setup.** For d⁸ in a tetrahedral field the ground term is ³T₁(F). The excited terms are ³T₂(F), ³A₂(F) and ³T₁(P). I take the two observed bands to be ³T₁(F)→³T₂ at 4 400 cm⁻¹ and ³T₁(F)→³T₁(P) at 15 500 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Dq from ³T₂.** In this geometry the ³T₂(F) energy is 2Dq and the ³T₁(F) energy is −6Dq. ³T₂ has no other term of its symmetry to mix with, so it stays pure. The transition energy is therefore 8Dq.\n\n8Dq = 4 400 cm⁻¹, so Dq = 550 cm⁻¹.\n\n**B from ³T₁(P).** The two ³T₁ terms mix through a 2×2 matrix, with energies measured from the ³F barycentre:\n\n| | ³T₁(F) | ³T₁(P) |\n|---|---|---|\n| ³T₁(F) | −6Dq | 6B |\n| ³T₁(P) | 6B | 15B + 6Dq |\n\nThe eigenvalues are E± = 7.5B ± √[(7.5B + 6Dq)² + 36B²]. The ³T₁(F)→³T₁(P) transition is the gap between them, 2√[(7.5B + 6Dq)² + 36B²].\n\nSet this gap to 15 500 cm⁻¹ with 6Dq = 3 300 cm⁻¹. Then the square root equals 7 750 cm⁻¹, and:\n\n(7.5B + 3 300)² + 36B² = 7 750²\n\nThis simplifies to 92.25B² + 49 500B − 49 172 500 = 0.\n\nThe positive root is B ≈ 510 cm⁻¹.\n\n**C.** Assuming C/B = 4.5 gives C ≈ 2 290 cm⁻¹. The ³T₁ matrix does not contain C, so this value does not enter the fit.\n\n**Result.** Dq ≈ 550 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 510 cm⁻¹. B is about half the free-ion value for Ni²⁺, which indicates strong covalency or a rough fit.\n\n{\"Dq\": 550, \"B\": 510, \"C\": 2290, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from 8Dq=4400 for the pure ³T₂ band; B from the 2x2 ³T₁(F)/³T₁(P) matrix fitted to 15500 cm⁻¹; C from the assumed C/B=4.5 and not constrained by the data.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1698,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1698,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","in